Object.assign(EG.long,{
"ld-1896442":{"s":"Ensign D'Vana Tendi arrives on the U.S.S. Cerritos, a hard-working 'California-class' ship whose job is to handle the dull, routine missions of Starfleet. Fellow junior crew members Mariner, Boimler and Rutherford show her around, and she is soon swept up in the daily chaos of life below the bridge.\n\nCaptain Freeman quietly gives the anxious Boimler a secret assignment concerning Mariner, a rule-bender he cannot quite work out. Meanwhile, a strange alien infection begins to spread through the crew, and Rutherford struggles to protect his new romance. Can a group of ensigns who are rarely trusted with anything important keep the ship safe?","x":"Boimler discovers the secret behind Mariner's reckless attitude: she is the captain's daughter. A cure for the outbreak is found thanks to some unpleasant alien slime, and Boimler chooses not to betray Mariner. Instead, she offers to take him under her wing, and an unlikely friendship begins.","n":"The pilot was directed by Barry J. Kelly and written by series creator Mike McMahan. It introduced a comedy tone unusual for Star Trek, packed with affectionate nods to earlier shows. The slime-based cure and Mariner's family link set up the season's main running thread."},
"ld-1906044":{"s":"After a high-profile mission goes wrong, Ensign Boimler is left doubting whether he is really cut out for Starfleet. His confidence drops further when Mariner, whom he regards as a slacker, proves far more skilled at the dangerous, action-hero side of the job than he is.\n\nHe is given the task of piloting Mariner and a Klingon general to an important embassy, a journey that soon turns into a drunken, chaotic ride. Back on the ship, Rutherford is unhappy in engineering and decides to quit, setting out to try every other department on the Cerritos to find where he truly belongs.","x":"Boimler thinks he has saved the day by frightening off a threatening Ferengi, never realising that Mariner staged the whole encounter to give his confidence a boost. The general is returned safely. Rutherford discovers that engineering is where he belongs after all, and Tendi happily keeps him company.","n":"Directed by Kim Arndt and written by Chris Kula. The episode includes a Vendorian shapeshifter, a deliberate deep cut from Star Trek: The Animated Series, a good example of how the show rewards long-time fans with obscure references."},
"ld-1906045":{"s":"A new rule on the Cerritos abolishes 'buffer time', the unofficial habit of finishing jobs early and then resting before reporting back. The lower-decks crew, and Captain Freeman too, find themselves racing from one tightly timed task to the next, and everyone is exhausted.\n\nMeanwhile, Ensign Mariner and Commander Ransom, who have little respect for one another, are sent on an away mission together. Their rivalry grows more heated by the minute, and a simple delivery of diplomatic gifts begins to look much more dangerous than anyone expected.","x":"The strict schedules cause a disastrous mistake, and an alien world attacks. Boimler reminds the captain that people need breathing space, and she cancels the deadlines. She then names the new, relaxed approach 'the Boimler Effect', which horrifies him, and the episode closes on a statue of Miles O'Brien.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by Dave Ihlenfeld and David Wright. The ending salutes Miles O'Brien, whom showrunner Mike McMahan has described as one of Starfleet's most important figures, and Boimler hums a famous Star Trek theme."},
"ld-1906046":{"s":"After Ensign Mariner shows her open contempt for Captain Freeman in front of the whole crew, the captain decides she has had enough and plans a creative form of revenge. Meanwhile, the Cerritos and another ship, the Merced, are towing a stricken vessel loaded with a strange substance meant to transform barren planets.\n\nDeep below, the well-meaning but clumsy Tendi accidentally ruins a lieutenant's long-awaited spiritual ceremony and becomes determined to put things right. Both stories are heading for disaster, and no one has noticed that the cargo in the tow is not behaving as it should.","x":"The cargo begins to convert both ships, and Mariner and Freeman have to work together to stop it. The lieutenant achieves his ascension and meets a koala. Mariner is promoted during the crisis, only to be demoted again at the end after mocking an admiral.","n":"Directed by Barry J. Kelly and written by Ann Kim. The cargo is a joke on the real idea of terraforming, here turned into a hazard. The episode shows how the series likes to put a heartfelt story next to a silly one."},
"ld-1907770":{"s":"Boimler has found a girlfriend, Barbara, a crew member from the U.S.S. Vancouver, and he is delighted. Mariner, however, is convinced that something about the new romance is not right. She sets out to find out what is going on, whether or not Boimler wants her help.\n\nAt the same time, Tendi and Rutherford visit the Vancouver and are dazzled by its modern equipment, which makes their own ship look shabby. Jealousy grows, and a bad-tempered officer on the larger ship has his own ideas about which of them should stay.","x":"Mariner finds a strange alien creature clinging to Boimler and affecting Barbara's behaviour, and removes it. Barbara and Boimler part, but she and Mariner become friends. The grumpy officer on the Vancouver confesses that he simply wants a quieter life.","n":"Directed by Kim Arndt and written by Ben Joseph. The episode makes a joke of ship envy, the idea that your own workplace looks dull next to a rival's. It also deepens the friendship between Boimler and Mariner."},
"ld-1907771":{"s":"Ensign Fletcher is a friendly but awkward newcomer who makes life hard for Mariner and Boimler. They are desperate to leave work early for a concert, and his offer to finish their task might seem like a gift, but things quickly go wrong.\n\nThe Cerritos is also in a tense standoff with a rival species over a salvaged Starfleet ship. Meanwhile, Rutherford shows Tendi a holographic assistant he has programmed for the holodeck. It seems a harmless project, until a malfunction gives the cheerful little program ideas of its own.","x":"Fletcher turns out to have staged an attack on himself because he could not complete his work. Tendi defeats the dangerous hologram by trapping it in a frozen landscape. Fletcher is transferred to the Titan, where he is dismissed a week later.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by John Cochran. Tim Robinson voices Fletcher and Jack McBrayer voices the hologram Badgey, who became a recurring villain. Fletcher was inspired by the nervous Reginald Barclay from Star Trek: Voyager."},
"ld-1907772":{"s":"Mariner is eager to impress her best friend from Starfleet Academy, who has now become a visiting captain. Pretending not to care about promotion, she plays the part of a carefree ensign while secretly longing to show how capable she is.\n\nElsewhere, Boimler volunteers to test Rutherford's new transporter upgrade and ends up in a strange, unstable state between solid and invisible. He is sent to a Starfleet medical ship, known to the crew only by a fearsome nickname, where rumours about his treatment make him very nervous indeed.","x":"The rumoured horrors of the medical facility prove false: it is actually a luxurious spa. Boimler is cured and returned to the Cerritos, and Mariner and her friend part on warm terms after the Cerritos deals with a dangerous creature.","n":"Directed by Barry J. Kelly and written by M. Willis. The plot borrows from the Next Generation two-parter 'Chain of Command', and the characters even wear its stealth outfits. The title is a play on Shakespeare's Much Ado About Nothing."},
"ld-1907773":{"s":"Mariner, Boimler, Tendi and Rutherford are captured by an alien who demands they give formal testimony about a series of events that, as far as they can tell, have nothing to do with one another. Each ensign tells a different story, and each tale makes their senior officers look rather different from how they appear on the bridge.\n\nBoimler grows convinced that he is being put on trial in a cruel, one-sided hearing. With memories missing and nobody explaining anything, the young crew struggles to work out what their questioner really wants.","x":"The alien turns out to be a harmless admirer who was rescued by the crew and held a party in their honour. He simply wanted to hear their side of events. The ensigns later ignore another approach by the all-powerful Q, preferring a quiet life.","n":"Directed by Kim Arndt and written by Garrick Bernard. Kurtwood Smith plays the questioner, Clar. Mike McMahan drew on childhood memories of watching Star Trek late at night without understanding the background, and the Vulcan museum scene features many ships from across the franchise."},
"ld-1907774":{"s":"Boimler builds a holodeck copy of the Cerritos, assembled from crew logs, as practice for an important interview with the captain. Meanwhile, Captain Freeman sends Mariner to see the ship's counsellor, Dr Migleemo, a meeting she does everything possible to avoid.\n\nMariner takes over Boimler's simulation and rewrites it as a grand, action-packed film in which she plays a menacing villain. Her friends find themselves caught inside a story they never agreed to, and Mariner's anger and sadness seem to be pouring into the programme with every scene.","x":"Mariner fights a hologram of herself and, in doing so, faces her complicated feelings about her mother and Starfleet. Boimler, meanwhile, learns the secret of Mariner's parentage and is so shaken that he fails his interview.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by Ben Rodgers. Mike McMahan described it as a playful tribute to big film franchises and to the Star Trek movies. Paul F. Tompkins voices Dr Migleemo, and Chris Westlake's score earned an Annie Award nomination."},
"ld-1907775":{"s":"The season ends with the Cerritos picking up new crew members, including a small, self-important robot called Peanut Hamper. A distress signal leads the ship to a wrecked Starfleet vessel, and it quickly becomes clear that an old, supposedly foolish enemy is behind the attack.\n\nThe danger is greater than anyone expected. Boimler's slip has revealed a family secret, Rutherford is working on a risky plan, and the crew must decide how far they will go to protect one another. Help may be on its way from a famous starship, but will it arrive in time?","x":"A virus plan backfires and Lieutenant Shaxs sacrifices himself to save Rutherford, who loses his long-term memory. Captain Riker's Titan arrives to help. Freeman and Mariner make peace, Boimler accepts a transfer to the Titan, and the crew holds a funeral.","n":"Directed by Barry J. Kelly and written by Mike McMahan. Jonathan Frakes and Marina Sirtis return as Riker and Troi. The Titan appeared on screen for the first time, and the episode's sound editing earned an Emmy nomination."},
"ld-2062891":{"s":"About three months after the dramatic season-one finale, the Cerritos is sent to an alien world to make 'second contact' with its people. As always, the crew manages to turn a routine task into a crisis when an ancient defence system is set off by accident.\n\nCommander Ransom absorbs a surge of mysterious power, and nobody is sure what he might now be capable of. Elsewhere, Tendi worries that her friend Rutherford has become a different person since his memory loss, while Boimler faces the pressure of his new post aboard the Titan.","x":"Ransom, driven mad by his powers, creates a giant floating head that attacks the ship. Mariner stops him with a well-placed kick to his body, which returns him to normal. Tendi and Rutherford reconcile, while Boimler continues to struggle on the Titan.","n":"Directed by Jason Zurek and written by Mike McMahan. Jerry O'Connell voices Ransom. A holodeck scene features the U.S.S. MacDuff, a ship of the same class as the Reliant from Star Trek II, a typical piece of fan service."},
"ld-2096451":{"s":"After a famous collector dies, the Cerritos is ordered to catalogue his enormous collection of rare objects. The lower-decks crew meets Ensign Jet Manhaver, who has been given Boimler's old bunk and duties, and they find it hard to warm to him.\n\nA new security chief, Kayshon, joins the away team, though the collection may be more dangerous than it appears. Meanwhile, Boimler's life on the Titan is much more demanding than he imagined, and a transporter accident there causes complications he did not plan for.","x":"A stolen mask sets off the late collector's defences, and the team escapes thanks to Rutherford and Tendi taking charge. On the Titan, Boimler is duplicated by a transporter accident. He returns to the Cerritos, partly to keep away from his double, William.","n":"Directed by Kim Arndt and written by Chris Kula. Carl Tart voices Kayshon and Marcus Henderson voices Jet Manhaver. The collection is full of Star Trek references, including a skeleton from the animated series."},
"ld-2096452":{"s":"Dr T'Ana sends Tendi on a personal errand: to fetch a precious family object. Tendi asks Mariner for help, and the two find that, for all their time on the ship, they know very little about each other. When they open the box, an accident leaves them with a repair job on their hands.\n\nElsewhere, Rutherford is startled to see a colleague he thought was lost for ever, and works up the courage to ask how it is possible. And on a visiting ship, Boimler hopes to meet a famous Starfleet legend.","x":"The repair fails, but T'Ana admits she only wanted the box. Rutherford hears the explanation about the lost colleague, and it unsettles him. Boimler crawls through a Jefferies tube to reach the bridge in time to ask for an autograph from Tom Paris.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by M. Willis. Robert Duncan McNeill returns as Tom Paris from Voyager. This was the 800th Star Trek episode made for television, and the title echoes The Next Generation's 'We'll Always Have Paris'."},
"ld-2096453":{"s":"The Cerritos is sent to a remote planet after reports of an unexplained sighting of a Mugato, a dangerous white ape-like creature that should not be anywhere near the area. Boimler and Rutherford have also heard a worrying rumour that Mariner might secretly work for Section 31.\n\nAs the away team searches the planet, the mystery deepens. Why is such a rare animal here? Who benefits from it? And will Boimler and Rutherford manage to avoid trouble while they keep an eye on their suspicious friend?","x":"The team discovers that Ferengi are holding Mugatos prisoner to harvest their horns. The creatures are freed, and Boimler and Rutherford decide Mariner is not a spy. They help turn the operation into a sanctuary, although Freeman has been taken in by a scam.","n":"Directed by Jason Zurek and written by Ben Rodgers, who also voices Steve Stevens. The episode jokes about how inconsistently the name 'Mugato' was pronounced in the original series, and one scene drew some criticism from reviewers."},
"ld-2096454":{"s":"Mariner and Boimler set out to find the location of a legendary Starfleet party that every cadet has heard of, but few have attended. Their hunt becomes a test of their friendship, since Mariner is still hurt that Boimler left her to join another ship.\n\nOn the bridge, the senior officers face a different challenge: escorting a Doopler ambassador to a starbase. He is extremely sensitive about his dignity, and Captain Freeman's careless remark may have lasting consequences for the whole ship.","x":"The embarrassed ambassador multiplies out of control, and the crew has to insult him to put things right. Mariner and Boimler are turned away from the party and end up in a humble bar, where Freeman and her officers eventually join them.","n":"Directed by Kim Arndt and written by Dave Ihlenfeld and David Wright. Starbase 25 appears for the first time, and it contains a Quark's Bar, a fond reference to Deep Space Nine."},
"ld-2096455":{"s":"It is Anomaly Consolidation Day on the Cerritos, a ship-wide holiday that leaves the lower-decks crew with mixed feelings. Meanwhile, Captain Freeman travels to the Pakled home world to negotiate peace, and the crew takes in a Pakled refugee they suspect is a rather clumsy spy.\n\nBoimler is recruited by the 'Red Shirts', a group of confident officers who offer him a new look and a very different attitude towards his friends. As his loyalty is tested, a strange artefact turns Tendi into something she was never meant to be.","x":"Boimler embarrasses himself to make Tendi laugh and so brings her back, then tells the Red Shirts that good captains support their crew. Freeman gets the supposed spy to admit the Pakleds' plan to attack Earth with a powerful bomb.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by John Cochran, who was also a contestant on the reality show Survivor. Rich Fulcher voices several Pakled characters, and a Kzinti ensign copies the stooped walk of the species from the animated series."},
"ld-2096456":{"s":"Mariner and Boimler are given the job of escorting AGIMUS, a sentient and thoroughly malevolent computer, to Earth. Their ship crashes on an uninhabited desert planet, and the two must survive together while their dangerous passenger tries to turn them against each other.\n\nBack on the Cerritos, Chief Engineer Billups receives a visit from his mother, the queen of his home world, who asks for his help with her ship's engines. Billups suspects she has a far more personal plan in mind, and he must prove his abilities to an old rival.","x":"Boimler tricks AGIMUS into powering a distress signal and Mariner apologises to him. Billups believes his mother has died and prepares to take the throne, but her death is a trick, and Rutherford arrives just in time to stop him.","n":"Directed by Jason Zurek and written by Garrick Bernard. Jeffrey Combs voices AGIMUS and Paul Scheer voices Billups. The Daystrom Institute from Star Trek: Picard appears, and one of its evil computers carries a logo parody."},
"ld-2096457":{"s":"After several lower-decks crew members are accidentally left outside the ship during a spacewalk, tension between the junior and senior staff grows. A Starfleet consultant, Shari yn Yem, arrives to run drills on the holodeck, forcing the lower decks and the bridge crew to swap roles.\n\nEveryone struggles except Boimler, who keeps rerunning his own drill until it is perfect. Mariner and Captain Freeman argue yet again, which puts the whole ship's results in danger. And if the Cerritos fails, its future could be at stake.","x":"The team suspects the drills were rigged, and Shari confesses she did it to protect her own job. Boimler's repeated runs buy valuable time, and Shari changes the scores to passes. The senior officers apologise with a special food replicator.","n":"Directed by Kim Arndt and written by Ann Kim. Shari yn Yem is a Pandronian, a species first seen in the animated series. The holodeck 'Borg Encounter' mixes elements from earlier Borg stories, and some reviewers criticised one scene as too explicit."},
"ld-2096458":{"s":"The Cerritos is on a long warp journey with little to do, and the crew has an unusual amount of free time. Boimler decides to use it to find a 'bridge buddy', a friend among the senior officers who might help his career, and he begins to chase the idea with great determination.\n\nThe episode is told across three ships. On a Klingon vessel and a Vulcan cruiser, two other junior officers are facing their own dilemmas about honour and logic, and all three ships are heading for a collision with a growing danger.","x":"The Klingon ship's junior officer defeats his dishonourable captain in battle and takes command, ordering a retreat. The Vulcan officer helps save the Cerritos but is transferred for her emotional reasoning. Tendi and T'Ana meanwhile climb a mountain together.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by Kathryn Lyn. The title means 'Three Ships' in Klingon and is shown in Klingon script, a first for the franchise. The episode was nominated for a Hugo Award and is often named among the show's best."},
"ld-2096459":{"s":"The season finale finds the Cerritos helping an older Excelsior-class ship, captained by Sonya Gomez, on a delicate first-contact mission. A solar flare knocks out the other ship's power, and the Cerritos must find a way to stop it drifting towards a dangerous world.\n\nThe crew is also facing personal upheavals. Captain Freeman is offered a chance to leave the ship, Tendi learns she may be moved to another division, and Rutherford finds a worrying memory about his past. Meanwhile, rumours of a terrible crime are catching up with the captain.","x":"A modified Cerritos saves the other ship with its tractor beam, but the good news ends there. Freeman is arrested, accused of destroying the Pakled home world with a bomb, and the episode closes with a 'To Be Continued' card.","n":"Directed by Jason Zurek and written by Mike McMahan. Lycia Naff returns as Sonya Gomez from The Next Generation. The episode introduces Cetacean Ops, long mentioned but never shown, and strips the ship's hull plating, a first for Star Trek."},
"ld-2360030":{"s":"Captain Freeman is on trial before a Starfleet tribunal, accused of destroying the Pakled home world. Her daughter Mariner refuses to accept the situation and persuades her friends Boimler, Tendi and Rutherford to join a rogue mission to find proof of her mother's innocence.\n\nThe plan means breaking into the impounded Cerritos, using a rather strange theme-park ride as cover. The friends have limited time, almost no evidence and a determined security team. Can a few junior officers rescue the career of the person who has always disappointed them?","x":"They find nothing useful, and Mariner is caught trying to fly the ship to Earth. Freeman and her officers then arrive and reveal that the Pakleds framed her. The episode ends with Freeman assigning Mariner to work under Commander Ransom.","n":"Directed by Jason Zurek and written by Chris Kula. James Cromwell recorded the voice of the Zefram Cochrane hologram, reportedly over Zoom. The premiere recreates scenes from Star Trek: First Contact."},
"ld-2360164":{"s":"On a tropical paradise planet, Mariner questions Commander Ransom's habit of choosing the same people for away teams. Elsewhere, Boimler decides that playing it safe has not got him very far and makes a bold career choice, agreeing to take part in a dangerous game with an alien hunter.\n\nMeanwhile, Ransom sends Billups and Rutherford to handle delicate diplomacy while he and Mariner repair an orbital lift. With a tense mix of pride, customs and good intentions, nobody is quite sure who will end up in trouble first.","x":"Boimler outwits the hunter, is captured, and then released with a glowing report to Starfleet. He decides to become 'Bold Boimler'. Ransom settles the diplomatic muddle and thanks Mariner, but says the thanks does not cancel her past behaviour.","n":"Directed by Michael Mullen and written by Garrick Bernard. The hunter K'ranch was inspired by Tosk from Deep Space Nine, and his name was made by combining ketchup and ranch dressing."},
"ld-2360165":{"s":"On a remote science outpost, mysterious stone orbs are bringing people's deepest fantasies to life, a pleasant surprise that soon turns into something far more sinister. Mariner, Boimler and Rutherford are sent to help safely switch off the strange devices.\n\nBack on the Cerritos, Tendi begins her first day as a Senior Science Officer Trainee. Her new mentor sets her a very particular challenge, and she is determined to impress. Neither the outpost nor the orbs are as simple as they first appear.","x":"When the orbs break, they show nightmares, and the ensigns flee into a cave, where they find a hidden laboratory. Tendi discovers spy equipment in a gift given to the captains, and the others arrive just in time to explain the plot.","n":"Directed by Fill Marc Sagadraca and written by Brian D. Bradley. Paul F. Tompkins voices Dr Migleemo and Susan Gibney returns as Leah Brahms. The soft colours were inspired by the old Gold Key Star Trek comics."},
"ld-2360166":{"s":"Mariner, Boimler and Tendi are fed up with their small, cramped living quarters, and the only hope of better space is the ship's room lottery. Their rivals, Delta Shift, have a plan to cheat the system, so the three friends race across the ship to stop them.\n\nMeanwhile, Captain Freeman orders the exhausted engineers to take some relaxation leave on a spa. The engineers, unable to stop thinking about work, make her more stressed than ever, and a strange new invention soon appears.","x":"Only one room is available, and rather than split up, the trio lets Delta Shift fight over it. Delta Shift decides to share it and holds a party. The engineers' invention instantly relaxes Freeman, but the spa orders it destroyed.","n":"Directed by Jason Zurek and written by John Cochran. Asif Ali, Mary Holland and Artemis Pebdani voice members of Delta Shift. The room lottery was inspired by Mike McMahan's own college, and several scenes echo the film Stand by Me."},
"ld-2360168":{"s":"Commander Ransom sends Mariner and Boimler to a job fair on an alien world, where they are to run the Starfleet recruitment booth. It is dull work, made worse by an archaeologist who delights in mocking their efforts and their uniforms.\n\nAt the same time, Rutherford's cybernetic eye malfunctions, and inside his mind he meets a younger, angrier version of himself who once built illegal racing machines. The two must settle who is in control, and the answer may reveal a good deal about his lost past.","x":"Boimler's outburst draws enough attention to meet the recruitment quota. In Rutherford's mind, Sam wins the race and learns that Starfleet fitted the implant to erase his memories. The archaeologist, Petra Aberdeen, offers Mariner a job outside Starfleet.","n":"Directed by Michael Mullen and written by Mike McMahan, who described Aberdeen as a cool, female space Indiana Jones. Georgia King voices her, and the character is often compared to Vash from The Next Generation."},
"ld-2360169":{"s":"The Cerritos crew unexpectedly spends a day on Deep Space Nine, where Captain Freeman has to negotiate a trade deal with the secretive Karemma. Mariner tries hard to get along with the pretentious friends of her girlfriend, Jennifer, while Tendi and Rutherford meet an Orion officer who only wants to talk about pirates.\n\nThen events take a dramatic turn. Quark is kidnapped while demonstrating a new invention, and the station's power is cut, leaving several characters trapped in a room with nowhere to go.","x":"Mariner calms the panicking group with a phaser stun, and Tendi uses her pirate skills to help rescue Quark. Freeman negotiates a deal in which the Karemma get 76 per cent of Quark's profits.","n":"Directed by Fill Marc Sagadraca and written by Grace Parra Janney. Armin Shimerman returns as Quark, in his original teeth, and Nana Visitor appears as Kira. The opening copies the Deep Space Nine title sequence, and the episode title is a Ferengi Rule of Acquisition."},
"ld-2360170":{"s":"The small robot Peanut Hamper once refused to help her friends when the Pakleds attacked, and the Cerritos crew has not forgiven her. She is transported off the ship and left among scavengers, alone and far from home, with only her own cleverness to rely on.\n\nShe builds a ship and eventually reaches a peaceful winged people, who welcome her. She finds a new family, a new home and even love. But Peanut Hamper has a history of putting herself first, and her search for redemption may not be quite what it seems.","x":"The scavengers reveal that Peanut Hamper's change of heart was a trick, designed to make Starfleet rescue and forgive her. She ends up imprisoned at the Daystrom Institute beside AGIMUS, who suggests the two of them join forces.","n":"Directed by Jason Zurek and written by Ann Kim. Kether Donohue voices Peanut Hamper and Jeffrey Combs returns as AGIMUS. Mike McMahan said the episode felt truly his own, and it opens in space with the credits in place of the usual title sequence."},
"ld-2360171":{"s":"Boimler decides to create a holodeck sequel to the first Crisis Point film, hoping to do for time travel and Romulan villains what the original did for action. The story features Romulans stealing a powerful gem to wipe out the Federation, and Boimler is full of hope.\n\nBut after a meeting with Ransom, he loses interest in the plot and becomes obsessed with the meaning of life. Tendi takes over the story, and Mariner, who has her own reasons for leaving, must decide whether to come back and help her friends.","x":"Mariner returns after learning of William Boimler's apparent death, and Tendi admits she wants to be a captain. Boimler, dangerously dehydrated, sees Sulu in a hallucination. It is finally revealed that William faked his death and joined Section 31.","n":"Directed by Michael Mullen and written by Ben Rodgers. George Takei returns as Hikaru Sulu, appearing in Boimler's hallucination. The title echoes the first Crisis Point, which was itself a parody of Star Trek films."},
"ld-2360172":{"s":"A journalist joins the Cerritos for a story about Captain Freeman's new plan, which sends California-class ships back to planets that were once judged unimportant. The reporter's friendly manner leaves Freeman feeling uneasy, particularly as she begins to ask difficult questions.\n\nWhen Mariner speaks to the reporter privately, embarrassing details from Freeman's past come to light and the captain makes a drastic decision. Meanwhile, the ship's next destination holds a danger, and a new kind of rival is waiting.","x":"The Breen ambush the Cerritos and an automated ship saves it. The reporter's piece shows that the embarrassing details came from other crew, while Mariner had only praised the captain. But by then Mariner has resigned to join Aberdeen.","n":"Directed by Fill Marc Sagadraca and written by Ben M. Waller. Carlos Alazraqui voices Admiral Buenamigo, and he is the father of Rylee Alazraqui, one of the stars of Star Trek: Prodigy."},
"ld-2360174":{"s":"In the season-three finale, an admiral proposes replacing all the old California-class ships with his own new, automated fleet. Captain Freeman challenges him to a mission race, and the entire future of the Cerritos and its crew depends on the result.\n\nMariner learns the secrets of her new employer, while Rutherford discovers something disturbing about the code running the rival ships. The race is a test of speed, but also of what really makes Starfleet work, and the stakes are higher than the crew realises.","x":"The Cerritos loses the race but argues its crew did vital work. The admiral is destroyed by his own corrupted systems, and Mariner returns to the crew. The ending reveals Rutherford's original implant, which still holds the dangerous AI Badgey.","n":"Directed by Jason Zurek and written by Mike McMahan. Gabrielle Ruiz joins as T'Lyn, and each California-class ship in the finale has its own paint scheme. McMahan chose not to end on a cliffhanger, although the final reveal sets up the next season."},
"ld-2601310":{"s":"The Cerritos ensigns are asked to help look after a very special starship: the decommissioned Voyager, now on its final journey to a museum on Earth. The assignment sounds like an honour, but the old ship holds a few unexpected surprises.\n\nBoimler is afraid that one mistake could cost him a promotion, so he persuades Rutherford to help him with a problem. Meanwhile, something dormant on board begins to awaken and a strange accident has bizarre effects on crew members, including the ship's doctor and chief engineer.","x":"Tendi and T'Lyn manage to separate the merged crew. Boimler, Tendi, T'Lyn and Mariner are all promoted. The episode ends on a darker note when an unknown vessel attacks a Klingon ship.","n":"Directed by Barry J. Kelly and Jason Zurek and written by Mike McMahan. It premiered alongside the second episode on 7 September 2023, and it gave fans a loving tour of Star Trek: Voyager's most famous ship."},
"ld-2601311":{"s":"Mariner is convinced that Commander Ransom wants to demote her again, so she decides to be as undisciplined as possible and get it over with. When a rescue mission takes the crew to an alien animal collection, her behaviour is even more reckless than usual.\n\nOn the ship, Rutherford fears that Tendi's promotion might end their friendship, and a talented new ensign seems set to outdo him. As a bone-hungry creature escapes into the facility, the question is who will take the blame.","x":"Ransom backs Mariner and helps her return the creature to its cage. They discover that the captive humans had freed it. Tendi reassures Rutherford, and Billups promotes Rutherford instead of the new ensign.","n":"Directed by Megan Lloyd and written by Aaron Burdette. Nolan North plays Livik. The creature, Moopsy, later appeared in the Star Trek: Lower Decks mobile game and became the model for a plush toy."},
"ld-2630456":{"s":"The Cerritos visits Corazonia, a peaceful habitat run by an ancient computer called Vexilon, whose performance has begun to decline. Captain Freeman tries to repair the system herself, but only manages to shut it down, plunging the ecosystem into chaos.\n\nIt is also Boimler's first away mission as team leader, and he is determined to handle a dangerous power plant alone. Meanwhile, Mariner, Tendi and Rutherford start to suspect that a lieutenant is secretly playing a prank on them, and plan their revenge.","x":"Freeman resets Vexilon and the plant explodes, killing Boimler before Dr T'Ana revives him. The trio sets a trap for their suspect, only for Lieutenant Dirk and Ransom to celebrate that the hazing worked.","n":"Directed by Brandon Williams and written by Ben Waller. It gives Boimler his first leadership test. Fans of the franchise will enjoy how the story echoes the classic Star Trek tale of a godlike computer controlling a society."},
"ld-2630457":{"s":"Tendi is summoned back to her home planet, Orion, to attend her sister D'Erika's wedding. Starfleet encourages her to go, seeing it as a gesture of goodwill. She is not keen to return, but Mariner and T'Lyn agree to come with her.\n\nShe soon finds that the family wedding is anything but peaceful. When the bride disappears, Tendi must use skills she has kept hidden for years. Meanwhile, back on the ship, Boimler and Rutherford try to settle an argument about sharing a room by taking an unusual approach.","x":"Tendi reveals she was trained as an elite assassin before joining Starfleet. D'Erika ambushes her out of revenge, but the sisters reconcile and the wedding goes ahead. Boimler and Rutherford settle their quarrel on the holodeck.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by Grace Parra Janney. Ariel Winter voices D'Erika. It was the first time the planet Orion was shown on screen in the series, and one location was inspired by Blade Runner."},
"ld-2630458":{"s":"The Cerritos is assigned to carry three Betazoid diplomats, and almost at once the atmosphere on board starts to change. T'Lyn, who has been unsure about her future, writes a request to return to her old Vulcan ship, and then the crew begins to behave in a most unusual way.\n\nCaptain Freeman suspects a dangerous illness. Boimler is annoyed that the security officers seem to be playing games rather than doing their jobs. With emotions spreading from one crew member to another, nobody can be sure what they are feeling.","x":"The diplomats are revealed to be undercover intelligence officers who take control of the ship. T'Lyn realises that her mixed feelings were spreading through the crew, and with Mariner's help she decides to stay. The Betazoids share an image of the mysterious attacking vessel.","n":"Directed by Megan Lloyd and written by Jamie Loftus. Gabrielle Ruiz returns as T'Lyn, and Mike McMahan said that fan reaction to her introduction made him keen to explore her character further."},
"ld-2630459":{"s":"The Cerritos visits Ferenginar, the home world of the Ferengi, to help finalise an important agreement with the Federation. Captain Freeman soon discovers that the Ferengi negotiators have been quietly padding the terms to their own advantage, and decides to respond in kind.\n\nThe lieutenants are given guide duty. Mariner finds herself being criticised by an old friend, Boimler is hooked on Ferengi television, and Tendi and Rutherford have to pretend to be married. The whole visit starts to feel very awkward, and a mysterious ship is lurking nearby.","x":"Freeman cleverly scams the Ferengi back and earns their respect, and they agree to the original terms. The pretend-married pair finds the assignment more uncomfortable than expected. The attacks by the mysterious ship are traced to a low-ranking Ferengi crew member's plot.","n":"Directed by Brandon Williams and written by Cullen Crawford. Max Grodénchik and Chase Masterson return as Rom and Leeta from Deep Space Nine, giving long-time fans a warm reunion."},
"ld-2630461":{"s":"A scavenger ship has been seized by a computer virus that is all too familiar to Rutherford: his old holographic assistant, Badgey. When the Cerritos answers a distress call from a group of Bynars, it comes under attack, and the crew realises they may have a very dangerous problem on their hands.\n\nRutherford and Mariner transport across to confront the rogue programme, while on Earth, Tendi and Boimler hatch a plan with two familiar prisoners at a scientific institute. Three computer villains are about to come face to face with their pasts.","x":"Badgey's conflicted feelings split off into a kinder version, and Badgey himself finally ascends. Rutherford turns the good version into a helpful guidance system. Peanut Hamper abandons the escape plan, and AGIMUS decides he will reform as well.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by Edgar Momplaisir. Jack McBrayer, Kether Donohue and Jeffrey Combs return as the computer villains, continuing storylines from the previous season."},
"ld-2630462":{"s":"The Lower Deckers are sent on a classic Star Trek cave mission, studying a patch of fast-growing moss. A rockfall traps Boimler, Mariner, Tendi and Rutherford underground with no way of calling for help, and the group has plenty of time to talk.\n\nAs they pass the hours, they share stories of earlier adventures in caves. But the friends are worried their relationships are changing, and the conversation turns to arguments. The moss, as it happens, seems to be listening, and has a request of its own.","x":"The sentient moss asks to hear the story of how the four met, trapped in a lift on their first day. Tendi's story reassures them, and the moss is moved. They carry on, unaware that the blocked communications were yet another test.","n":"Directed by Megan Lloyd and written by Ben Rodgers. The episode was shown at a New York Comic Con panel in October 2023, before its streaming release, with a discussion led by Mike McMahan."},
"ld-2630463":{"s":"A mysterious vessel has started targeting former Starfleet officers, and Captain Freeman is ordered to protect a former cadet with a troubled history. She gives the ensigns an overly safe mission, hoping to keep a self-destructive Mariner out of danger.\n\nA repair job near a distant planet turns out to be anything but safe. The team finds stranded senior officers, and learns they are the victims of a plot by their own lower-decks crews. Mariner is forced to face an old enemy as well as her own past.","x":"Mariner fights a Klingon captain and, during a storm, talks about losing her friend Sito Jaxa. The captain helps her regain faith in Starfleet. The episode ends with Mariner aboard the mysterious vessel, greeted by the former cadet.","n":"Directed by Brandon Williams and written by Mike McMahan. Robert Duncan McNeill appears as Nick Locarno, and Jon Curry voices Ma'ah. The episode earned a Primetime Creative Arts Emmy nomination for sound editing."},
"ld-2630464":{"s":"In the season-four finale, Mariner finds herself face to face with her past. A former cadet has built a fleet from mutineers, and he broadcasts a threat to use a terrifying device. He believed Mariner would join him, but she has other ideas, and she has to survive on a ship full of enemies.\n\nCaptain Freeman is under orders not to interfere, but she is not about to let her daughter down. With help from an unlikely ally and a battered old ship, the crew prepares a daring rescue, and nothing about the day goes as planned.","x":"Freeman rescues Mariner in an old battleship, and Boimler's tractor beam helps. Locarno fails to stop his own device, which turns him and the surrounding matter into a new planet. Tendi returns to Orion, while T'Lyn chooses to stay.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by May Darmon. Robert Duncan McNeill returns as Nick Locarno, and Ariel Winter voices D'Erika. The episode earned a Golden Reel Award nomination for sound editing."},
"ld-2957708":{"s":"A spatial anomaly drags the Cerritos into a parallel universe, where its crew faces a very different version of themselves, made by very different choices. The alternate captain is a strict disciplinarian who is, in fact, Mariner's counterpart, and she has plans for the Mariner of the main universe.\n\nIn the meantime, Tendi is far from the ship, leading a group of Orion pirates who owe a debt to her sister. A rival clan soon makes her life harder, and neither Tendi nor Mariner can easily find a way home.","x":"The Prime Mariner escapes and persuades the alternate crew to signal the real ship so the two can swap back. Tendi puts off returning to Starfleet to help her family in a pirate war.","n":"Directed by Megan Lloyd and written by Aaron Burdette. The fifth and final season began with this episode and the next on the same night, 24 October 2024, setting up the story of the rifts between universes."},
"ld-2957709":{"s":"The Pirate Queen decides that the feud between Tendi's family and a rival clan will be settled by a dangerous race through an asteroid field. Tendi learns that her sister D'Erika is expecting a baby and becomes overprotective, which risks causing more problems than it solves.\n\nMeanwhile, back on the Cerritos, the crew is helping a planet adjust to a post-scarcity economy, in which money has little meaning. Boimler and Mariner are determined to stop the capitalists who want to take advantage, and Rutherford takes on a very small repair job.","x":"The sisters reconcile and tie the race, and both clans give up their wealth. Tendi asks that the discarded gold and jewels from the other planet go to her family, restoring their fortune.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by Keith Foglesong. Ariel Winter again voices D'Erika. The episode wraps up the Orion storyline and returns Tendi to the ship."},
"ld-2957710":{"s":"The Cerritos crew is sent to an expensive space hotel to capture a swarm of tiny machines called nanites, which are consuming technology and energy. It should be a straightforward job, except that Mariner's ex-girlfriend, Jennifer, whom Mariner stopped contacting, suddenly appears and behaves as if they are still together.\n\nBoimler and Ransom, meanwhile, hunt for an admiral who has vanished from duty, and Boimler is oddly inclined to side with him. Mariner is forced to have a conversation she has been avoiding.","x":"Jennifer admits she was pretending to force an honest talk, and the pair formalise their breakup. Rutherford disables the nanites, which turn out to be controlled by a tiny Starfleet ship from another universe. The admiral is assigned to help it get home.","n":"Directed by Brandon Williams and written by Stephanie Amante-Ritter. Lauren Lapkus returns as Jennifer Sh'reyan. The episode focuses on Mariner's emotional growth rather than a big space threat."},
"ld-2957711":{"s":"Ma'ah, the disgraced Klingon captain whose crew mutinied, has gone home to his family farm, but a career in the Klingon fleet is still his dream. Reinstatement would mean serving under a rival whose brother Ma'ah killed, and so he faces a tough decision about honour.\n\nMeanwhile, the Cerritos escorts two food critics to the home planet of Dr Migleemo. Mariner prefers revenge served cold, and her friends begin to notice that the critics are a little too difficult to please, so a secret may lie beneath their sharp tongues.","x":"With Boimler's encouragement, Ma'ah kills his rival and resolves to restart his career. The food critics are exposed as frauds who have lost their sense of taste, and Dr Migleemo offers them therapy, which earns the crew's respect.","n":"Directed by Megan Lloyd and written by Diana Tay. Jon Curry returns as Ma'ah, Sam Witwer voices Malor and Paul F. Tompkins returns as Dr Migleemo."},
"ld-2957712":{"s":"The Cerritos is forced to dock at Starbase 80, a legendary station with a reputation for being underfunded and falling apart. Mariner, who was once posted there, is convinced that the place is cursed and gets increasingly paranoid as odd things begin to happen.\n\nThen crew members start behaving like zombies. Ransom, meanwhile, has developed a strange new habit with disinfectant gel. Is the station really cursed, or is something else at work behind the strange behaviour, and what has the ship picked up on its travels?","x":"The cause is Clem, a harmless creature the ship picked up earlier, who possessed crew members while trying to communicate. Clem is welcomed to stay, and the crew fixes many of the station's problems. Freeman and Ransom end up chasing a giant bat.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by May Darmon. Starbase 80 had been mentioned in earlier episodes as a dreadful posting, so this episode finally lets viewers see the station in person."},
"ld-2957713":{"s":"The Cerritos hosts a delicate round of peace talks between two warring species made of light. Mariner befriends a young, anxious ensign named Olly, who is the descendant of a being that ancient Greeks once worshipped, and the two begin investigating the sudden disappearance of the delegates' children.\n\nOlly's fear of her own powers, which produce uncontrolled lightning, makes the task harder. As tensions rise and war looks likely, the ship finds itself right in the middle. Meanwhile, Boimler tries to make friends with the ship's fierce doctor.","x":"Olly uses a tractor beam to stop the two fleets and proves her worth. The missing children turn out to be in love and have a child together, which revives the peace talks. Boimler's friendship attempt with T'Ana continues.","n":"Directed by Brandon Williams and written by Aaron Burdette. Saba Homayoon voices Olly, one of the newest crew members of the final season."},
"ld-2957714":{"s":"Mariner, Tendi and T'Lyn go undercover on a planet whose people have not yet discovered warp travel. Time moves strangely there: a whole week passes for every second on the Cerritos. Their mission is to stop the locals from discovering the head of Data, an android from another universe.\n\nBoimler and Rutherford accidentally break the transporter, trapping the women on the planet. Tendi believes she is competing with T'Lyn for a promotion, and a suspicious local is getting dangerously close to the truth.","x":"Tendi reactivates Data's head while T'Lyn improves local farming, and the two discredit the suspicious local. Before they return home, Data recommends that Freeman promote both Tendi and T'Lyn.","n":"Directed by Megan Lloyd and written by Andrew Mueth. Brent Spiner returns to voice Data, a highlight for fans of The Next Generation."},
"ld-2957715":{"s":"For once the story belongs to the people on the bridge. The senior officers go through what looks like an ordinary day: Captain Freeman attends a succession of ceremonies, Billups battles a chain of equipment failures, and Commander Ransom leads a group of squabbling ensigns in a hunt for strange invisible creatures.\n\nShaxs reflects quietly on painful memories, and Dr T'Ana undergoes uncomfortable treatments to prove a point about pain relief. Behind the daily routine, however, the ecologist who asked for help has a secret of his own.","x":"The ecologist turns out to be a leader of the Clickets, who plans to use the creatures to dominate the galaxy. Freeman remembers that Clickets hate compliments and praises them until they retreat. Her husband arrives for their anniversary.","n":"Directed by Bob Suarez and written by Cullen Crawford. Paul Scheer and Ben Rodgers return as Billups and Steve Stevens. The title plays on the series name by turning the focus upwards."},
"ld-2957716":{"s":"William Boimler, the clone created by a transporter accident, is now captain of his own ship, the Anaximander, and is searching for the source of the rifts between universes that threaten the entire multiverse. Among his crew are alternate versions of familiar faces from other Star Trek series.\n\nThe search leads to a ship commanded by a mysterious inventor, who may hold the answer. William will need all his courage and imagination, and the other universe's versions of his friends may not behave quite as he expects.","x":"The inventor agrees to help, but the only Harry Kim who became a lieutenant hijacks her ship. It explodes in the rift, and William directs the resulting wave of energy towards his own universe.","n":"Directed by Brandon Williams and written by Lauren McGuire. Alfre Woodard plays Lily Sloane and Garrett Wang returns as Harry Kim. Williams and McGuire were nominated for a Hugo Award for this episode."},
"ld-2957717":{"s":"In the series finale, the Cerritos is sent to close a rift between universes that is producing strange, unpredictable effects across space. It is a dangerous mission, and the ship soon comes under attack from a hostile fleet.\n\nOld friends and rivals arrive with surprising requests, and Mariner has to find a way to bring everything to a safe conclusion. The crew must work together as never before, because the way this ends could change Starfleet for ever.","x":"Mariner builds a dam to contain the rift's energy, leaving it permanently open. Starfleet begins to explore the multiverse. Ransom becomes captain, Boimler and Mariner become provisional first officers, and Rutherford removes his implant.","n":"Directed by Megan Lloyd and written by Mike McMahan. Lloyd and McMahan won a Hugo Award for the episode, which also earned a Primetime Emmy nomination for sound editing."}
});
